# MSA Schedule 4 — Chronic failure

> Module ChronicFailure

L-6.1 gives the Customer a termination right on a **pattern** of failures
rather than on any single one, and the pattern has two independent triggers:
three consecutive Measurement Periods below 99.90%, or any four Measurement
Periods in a rolling twelve-month period. Either suffices, so a history can
satisfy the second without ever satisfying the first. L-6.2 then puts a 30-day
exercise window on the right, running from the end of the Measurement Period
which gives rise to it, and lapses it per occurrence.

A history can complete the pattern many times over, and L-6.2 lapses the right
"in respect of **that occurrence**". The unit of both the window and the lapse
is therefore the individual occurrence, so this scope assesses **every**
occurrence the history contains, each against its own window, and reports the
aggregate answers as facts about that list rather than about one chosen
occurrence.

The history is a list of Measurement Periods. Because L-2.1 makes a
Measurement Period a calendar month, each period is identified by the first
day of its month, which makes "consecutive" and "rolling twelve months" exact
month arithmetic rather than an approximation in days — and adding a month to
the first day of a month is never ambiguous, so no rounding mode is needed.
The availability figure on each period is the L-2.3 figure, already rounded to
two decimal places.

There are no judgement inputs. Every term is arithmetic over the history, a
date of record, or the fact of whether notice was given.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration structure PeriodAvailability:
  # L-2.1: the calendar month, identified by its first and last day.
  data period_start content date
  data period_end content date
  # L-2.3: the Availability Percentage, rounded to two decimal places, on the
  # 0-100 scale.
  data availability_percentage content decimal

declaration structure OccurrenceAssessment:
  # One occurrence of the L-6.1 right: the Measurement Period which gave rise
  # to it, that occurrence's own L-6.2 window, and that occurrence's own
  # state as at the assessment date. L-6.2 lapses the right "in respect of
  # that occurrence", so every one of these fields is per-occurrence.
  data trigger_period_start content date
  data trigger_period_end content date
  # The window opens the day after the triggering period ends and closes on
  # the thirtieth day after it.
  data window_opens content date
  data exercise_deadline content date
  data arisen content boolean
  data window_open content boolean
  data exercised_by_notice content boolean
  data lapsed content boolean
  data exercisable content boolean

declaration scope ChronicFailureRight:
  input availability_history content list of PeriodAvailability
  input assessment_date content date
  input notice_given content boolean
  input notice_date content date
  # Every entry of the history below the Service Level, as given.
  output failing_periods content list of PeriodAvailability
  # Those entries reduced to one per Measurement Period, which is the unit
  # L-6.1 counts in.
  output failing_measurement_periods content list of PeriodAvailability
  output consecutive_trigger content boolean
  output rolling_trigger content boolean
  output right_arisen content boolean
  output completing_periods content list of PeriodAvailability
  # L-6.2 confines a lapse to "that occurrence", so every occurrence is
  # assessed in its own right and the lists below are views of that
  # assessment rather than a single chosen occurrence.
  output occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  output arisen_occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  output live_occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  output exercised_occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  output lapsed_occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  # The occurrence whose dates the reported window describes; a reporting
  # convenience only, since no legal conclusion below rests on it.
  output governing_occurrences content list of OccurrenceAssessment
  output trigger_period_end content optional of date
  output notice_period_days content integer
  output early_termination_charges_payable content boolean
  output exercise_deadline content optional of date
  # Whether the notice of record falls inside both ends of the L-6.2 window
  # of some occurrence.
  output notice_exercises_the_right content boolean
  output right_lapsed content boolean
  output right_exercisable content boolean
```

## L-6.1 The two triggers

| MSA-SCH4 L-6.1 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:102)
|
| Where the Availability Percentage is less than 99.90% in each of three
| consecutive Measurement Periods, or in any four Measurement Periods in a rolling
| twelve-month period, the Customer may terminate the Agreement in respect of the
| affected Service on 30 days' written notice, without liability for early
| termination charges.

"Less than 99.90%" is strict on both triggers, matching L-3.1: a period at
exactly 99.90% is not a failing period and breaks a run.

Both limbs of L-6.1 count **Measurement Periods**, and L-2.1 makes a
Measurement Period a calendar month. A calendar month is one Measurement
Period however many availability reports describe it, so the history has to be
reduced to one entry per month before anything is counted. A Supplier that
issues a report and then restates it leaves two entries for the same month in
the Customer's record, and nothing in Schedule 4 makes a restated month a
second Measurement Period.

An earlier version counted list **entries** in the rolling limb — the number
of failing entries whose `period_start` fell in the twelve-month window — so
two failing months, each reported twice, satisfied the four-period limb and
handed the Customer a right to terminate without early termination charges on
facts that give none. The consecutive limb was never affected, because it
matches on `period_start - 1 month` and `- 2 month` and so is insensitive to
duplicates; the two limbs therefore disagreed about the same facts, which is
what exposed it. Found by the adversarial reviewer; see
tests/counterexamples/CE-0025.yaml.

`failing_measurement_periods` is that reduction, keeping the first entry
recorded for each month. Which entry survives cannot change any answer below:
every field used downstream — `period_start` and `period_end` — is a property
of the calendar month itself, and the only field that varies between
restatements, `availability_percentage`, has already done its work in the
filter. `failing_periods` remains an output so that the full record the
Customer supplied stays visible next to the reduction of it.

Both triggers are expressed as tests on a single **completing** period — the
period at whose end the pattern is complete. That is the period L-6.2 calls
"the Measurement Period which gives rise to it", so computing the triggers
this way yields the exercise window as well as the right.

  * consecutive: the period fails, and so do the two calendar months
    immediately before it. Three is the stated number, so two consecutive
    failures are not enough and a gap of one good month resets the run.
  * rolling: the period fails, and counting back across the twelve months
    ending with it there are at least four failing periods. Four is the stated
    number, and eleven months back from the completing month is the first
    month of the twelve-month window.

The two are independent disjuncts, not exceptions to one another, so
`right_arisen` is a disjunction. The remaining limbs of L-6.1 — 30 days'
written notice and no liability for early termination charges — are stated
constants.

```catala
scope ChronicFailureRight:
  definition failing_periods equals
    list of p among availability_history such that
      p.availability_percentage < 99.90

  definition failing_measurement_periods equals
    combine all p among failing_periods in acc
    initially []
    with (if (exists q among acc such that q.period_start = p.period_start)
          then acc
          else acc ++ [p])

  definition consecutive_trigger equals
    number of (list of p among failing_measurement_periods such that
      ((exists q among failing_measurement_periods such that
          q.period_start = p.period_start - 1 month)
       and (exists r among failing_measurement_periods such that
          r.period_start = p.period_start - 2 month))) > 0

  definition rolling_trigger equals
    number of (list of p among failing_measurement_periods such that
      (number of (list of q among failing_measurement_periods such that
         ((q.period_start <= p.period_start)
          and (q.period_start >= p.period_start - 11 month)))) >= 4) > 0

  definition right_arisen equals consecutive_trigger or rolling_trigger

  definition completing_periods equals
    list of p among failing_measurement_periods such that
      (((exists q among failing_measurement_periods such that
           q.period_start = p.period_start - 1 month)
        and (exists r among failing_measurement_periods such that
           r.period_start = p.period_start - 2 month))
       or (number of (list of q among failing_measurement_periods such that
             ((q.period_start <= p.period_start)
              and (q.period_start >= p.period_start - 11 month)))) >= 4)

  definition notice_period_days equals 30

  definition early_termination_charges_payable equals false
```

## L-6.2 Every occurrence has its own window

| MSA-SCH4 L-6.2 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:108)
|
| The right in L-6.1 must be exercised within 30 days after the end of the
| Measurement Period which gives rise to it, failing which it lapses in respect of
| that occurrence.

"The Measurement Period which gives rise to it" is a completing period. A
history can contain more than one, and the closing words of L-6.2 confine the
lapse to "that occurrence" — the occurrence whose own window expired — and so
expressly preserve every other one.

An earlier version took `trigger_period_end` as the **minimum** period end
over all completing periods, so the whole scope answered about the earliest
occurrence ever recorded. A cluster of failures in early 2024 whose window
closed on 30 April 2024 therefore destroyed the right arising from a fresh
cluster in 2025, and notice served five days after that 2025 occurrence arose
was reported out of time by fifteen months. Found by the adversarial
reviewer; see tests/counterexamples/CE-0007.yaml.

The version after that collapsed the occurrences differently but still
collapsed them: it chose one occurrence — the latest live one, failing that
the latest arisen one — and tested the notice against that occurrence alone.
Four consecutive months below the Service Level produce two occurrences, one
completing in March and one in April. A notice inside the March occurrence's
window exercised the March right when the question was asked in April, and
then stopped having exercised anything once the March window closed and the
April occurrence became the one chosen. The answer moved with the date the
question was asked rather than with the facts L-6.2 makes relevant, and a
right that had been validly exercised was reported as lapsed. Found by the
adversarial reviewer; see tests/counterexamples/CE-0024.yaml.

So there is no chosen occurrence. `occurrences` assesses each completing
period against its own window:

  * `arisen`: the occurrence exists only from the end of its own triggering
    period, because L-6.1 confers the right on the expiry of that period. An
    occurrence whose period ends after the assessment date has not arisen and
    is not assessed.
  * `window_open`: it has arisen and the assessment date is not past the
    thirtieth day after its triggering period ended.
  * `exercised_by_notice`: the notice of record falls inside both ends of
    **this** occurrence's window.
  * `lapsed`: it has arisen, its window has closed, and no notice exercised
    it. This is the lapse L-6.2 confines to "that occurrence".
  * `exercisable`: it has arisen and either its window is still open or a
    notice exercised it. An exercised right does not become unexercisable
    when its window closes; the window governs the act of exercise, not the
    life of the right once exercised.

`governing_occurrences` exists only so that the scope can still report a
single window in `trigger_period_end` and `exercise_deadline` for a reader who
wants one: the occurrences a notice actually exercised, failing that the live
ones, failing that those that have arisen, and `trigger_period_end` is the
latest of whichever of those sets is reached. It is a description, not a
decision — every legal conclusion below is computed over the whole list of
occurrences, and no answer changes if this ordering is read differently.

```catala
scope ChronicFailureRight:
  definition occurrences equals
    map each p among completing_periods to
      (let has_arisen equals p.period_end <= assessment_date in
       let deadline equals p.period_end + 30 day in
       let is_open equals has_arisen and (assessment_date <= deadline) in
       let exercised equals
         has_arisen
         and notice_given
         and (notice_date > p.period_end)
         and (notice_date <= deadline)
       in
       OccurrenceAssessment {
         -- trigger_period_start: p.period_start
         -- trigger_period_end: p.period_end
         -- window_opens: p.period_end + 1 day
         -- exercise_deadline: deadline
         -- arisen: has_arisen
         -- window_open: is_open
         -- exercised_by_notice: exercised
         -- lapsed: has_arisen and (not is_open) and (not exercised)
         -- exercisable: has_arisen and (is_open or exercised)
       })

  definition arisen_occurrences equals
    list of o among occurrences such that o.arisen

  definition live_occurrences equals
    list of o among occurrences such that o.window_open

  definition exercised_occurrences equals
    list of o among occurrences such that o.exercised_by_notice

  definition lapsed_occurrences equals
    list of o among occurrences such that o.lapsed

  definition governing_occurrences equals
    if (number of exercised_occurrences) > 0 then exercised_occurrences
    else if (number of live_occurrences) > 0 then live_occurrences
    else if (number of arisen_occurrences) > 0 then arisen_occurrences
    else occurrences

  definition trigger_period_end equals
    if right_arisen then
      Present content
        (maximum of
           (map each o among governing_occurrences to o.trigger_period_end)
         or if list empty then assessment_date)
    else Absent

  definition exercise_deadline equals
    match trigger_period_end with pattern
    -- Absent: Absent
    -- Present content d: Present content (d + 30 day)
```

## L-6.2 Exercise inside the window, and lapse

| MSA-SCH4 L-6.2 (004-msa-sla-credits.md:108)
|
| The right in L-6.1 must be exercised within 30 days after the end of the
| Measurement Period which gives rise to it, failing which it lapses in respect of
| that occurrence.

L-6.2 fixes a window with two ends, not just a deadline. The right "must be
exercised within 30 days after the end of the Measurement Period which gives
rise to it": the window opens when that period ends, because L-6.1 confers the
right only on the expiry of the third failing period, and closes on the
thirtieth day after it. "Within 30 days" is inclusive of the thirtieth, as in
L-5.1, so the window is days 1 to 30 after the end of that period.

An earlier version tested only the upper bound — `if notice_given then
notice_date > deadline else assessment_date > deadline` — so any notice
bearing a date on or before the deadline was treated as a good exercise,
including one dated before the third failing period had even ended, and the
right was then reported as exercisable indefinitely. A notice cannot exercise
a right that did not exist when it was served. Found by the adversarial
reviewer; see tests/counterexamples/CE-0008.yaml.

The aggregate answers are therefore quantified over the occurrences, and a
notice is tested against the occurrence whose window contains it (CE-0024):

  * `notice_exercises_the_right`: the notice fell inside the window of **some**
    occurrence. It exercises that occurrence's right, and L-6.2 says nothing
    that lets a later occurrence displace it.
  * `right_lapsed`: the right has lapsed only where every occurrence that has
    arisen has lapsed. One occurrence lapsing leaves the others untouched,
    which is exactly what "in respect of that occurrence" provides. Where no
    occurrence has yet arisen there is nothing that could have lapsed, so the
    condition also requires at least one.
  * `right_exercisable`: some occurrence is exercisable — its window is still
    open, or a notice validly exercised it.

The lapse exception repeats the parent's condition `right_arisen`, since a
right that never arose cannot lapse — a Catala exception does not inherit its
parent's condition, and the base case here is the unconditional "has not
lapsed".

```catala
scope ChronicFailureRight:
  definition notice_exercises_the_right equals
    notice_given and ((number of exercised_occurrences) > 0)

  label l6_2_not_lapsed definition right_lapsed equals false

  definition right_exercisable equals
    right_arisen
    and ((number of (list of o among occurrences such that o.exercisable)) > 0)
```
